<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">10 January in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">11 events that happened on 10 January, from 49 BCE to 1984.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/49-bce">49 BCE</a> <span class="tih-ago">2,074 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Julius Caesar crosses the Rubicon</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Leading a legion across the small river that marked the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Caesar defied the Senate and made civil war inevitable. 'The die is cast', he reportedly said. The war ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1072">1072 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">954 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Normans under Robert Guiscard capture Palermo</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After a five-month siege, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> and his brother Roger took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> from its Muslim rulers. The conquest paved the way for the Norman Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, a meeting place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Greek and Arab cultures.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1430">1430 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">596 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Philip the Good founds the Order of the Golden Fleece</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At his wedding celebrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> created an exclusive order of knighthood. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and survives today in Spanish and Austrian branches.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1776">1776 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">250 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Thomas Paine publishes 'Common Sense'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Paine's plain-spoken pamphlet argued that the American colonies should declare independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. It sold tens of thousands of copies within months and helped turn opinion towards independence.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1840">1840 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">186 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Uniform Penny Post introduced in Britain</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Rowland Hill's reform let a letter travel anywhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for one penny, paid by the sender. Letter volumes soared, and the Penny Black, the first adhesive stamp, followed in May.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1861">1861 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">165 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Florida secedes from the Union</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>'s convention voted 62 to 7 to leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the third state to do so after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>. It joined the Confederate States the following month.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1901">1901 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">125 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Spindletop gusher erupts in Texas</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Oil burst from the Lucas well at Spindletop near Beaumont, spouting over 40 metres high for nine days. The strike created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> oil boom and made petroleum cheap enough to fuel the motor age.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1920">1920 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">106 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">League of Nations comes into existence</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> came into force, and with it the Covenant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, the first permanent organisation for keeping world peace. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> never joined.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1946">1946 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">80 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">First UN General Assembly meets in London</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Delegates from 51 nations gathered at Methodist Central Hall, Westminster, for the first session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a>. The UN later moved its headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1954">1954 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">72 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">BOAC Comet airliner breaks up over the Mediterranean</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A de Havilland Comet, the world's first jet airliner, disintegrated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>, killing all 35 aboard. Investigations traced the disaster to metal fatigue, transforming aircraft design and testing.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1984">1984 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">42 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">United States and the Holy See establish full diplomatic relations</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">President Reagan's administration and the Vatican agreed to exchange ambassadors, ending a gap in formal relations since 1867. The move reflected close cooperation with Pope John Paul II during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li></ol>
